Shopping Hours

Many visitors expect shopping malls and stores to be open as usual or even longer than usual on public holidays. New Zealand has an approach that may surprise visitors, as on Christmas, Good Friday, Easter, and public holidays, a significant part of the trade temporarily ceases to operate. It can be difficult to find an open shopping mall or big store even in the capital of NZ. This comes as a surprise to travelers who are used to shopping at any time. Locals try to buy everything they need in advance before the holidays. People should take this into account when planning their trip. The most noticeable restrictions apply to:

- Shopping malls;

- Clothing stores;

- Part of supermarkets;

- Restaurants and cafes;

- Entertainment venues;

- Local family businesses.

Life in cities becomes calmer and more measured during public holidays. Many residents prefer to spend time with family, go out into nature, or meet with friends at home. For New Zealanders, this is considered an important part of holiday traditions and relaxation. To foreigners, this type of approach may seem unusual because in CIS or EU countries, holidays are often accompanied by sales and high trading activity.

