Rules for purchasing and consuming alcohol

Alcohol regulations are structured in a way that the main nuance isn't on the prohibition of consumption. It lies in the control of sales in New Zealand. This nice country allows people to buy alcohol from the age of 18 officially, while this age is considered key in the legislation. There is no separate, clear minimum age for drinking alcohol in private, if this happens under the supervision of parents or legal guardians. In stores and bars, there is a strict check of documents at the checkout and at the entrance. Employees are required to refuse a sale if there is a suspicion that a drink may be handed over to a minor. The presence of a teenager nearby may be a reason to refuse to sell alcohol to an adult, but rules of this type are aimed at reducing minors' access to alcohol.

Control in the alcohol sector is quite strict, while sellers are responsible for any violations. The sale can be immediately canceled if there is any doubt that alcohol is being purchased for someone else. This applies to situations when an adult comes to the store with minors. In these cases, the cashier has the right to refuse to complete the purchase without giving a reason. This practice is common and supported in land-based casinos or restaurants. Locals believe that this helps reduce early alcohol consumption among young people.

Free access to nature

In New Zealand, access to nature is one of the freest in the world, as anyone can verify by actually using the areas. Most beaches, lakes, mountain routes, and nature trails are open to everyone without fees, which makes traveling around the country as accessible as possible. In many cases, areas that in other countries might be private or restricted remain open to public use here. This creates a feeling of constant proximity to nature even within settlements. The state supports the principle of free access to natural resources and tourist routes. The most common types of natural areas open to everyone are:

- Public beaches along the coast;

- Mountain hiking trails and tracks;

- Lakes and rivers for recreation;

- National parks and conservation areas;

- Forests with open hiking trails;

- Coastal natural areas.

This approach allows people to travel without problems and play casino games with friends in nature without permits or fees. Many routes are designed so that they can be used by both tourists and locals. This creates a strong connection between the population and the environment. At the same time, there are rules of conduct that prohibit harming nature or leaving garbage. Violation of these rules can lead to fines or access restrictions.
